## 1. Introduction
Anthropogenic activities have resulted in an exponential increase in pollutants in the environment. Some of the problematic contaminants discharged into the environment due to human activities include inor­ ganic pollutants such as heavy metals (Pavithra et al., 2021; Sun et al., 2021; Thanthri et al., 2021) as well as organic pollutants such as dyes (El-Zawahry et al., 2021; Haroun et al., 2021; Reghioua et al., 2021), pesticides (Abukhadra et al., 2021; Mohammadi et al., 2021; Singh et al., 2020) and antibiotics (Jia, Bai, et al., 2021; Liu, Nie, & Yu, 2021; Yaqubi et al., 2021). These pollutants have adverse effects on the environment and therefore their effective removal from the ecosystems is imperative.
Adsorptive removal of inorganic and organic pollutants from the environment has gained popularity over the years (Palacio et al., 2020; Shafaati et al., 2020; R. Tang, Wang, et al., 2021). This can be attributed to the fact that it is a less sophisticated technique and is relatively cheap. The success of the adsorptive removal of pollutants from the environ­ ment hinges on the availability of materials that have good adsorptive properties. Some of these materials include polydopamine (W. Fan, Yang, et al., 2021; Feng et al., 2021; Musarurwa & Tawanda Tavengwa, 2021; S. Shi, Xu, et al., 2021; Zhang et al., 2021), activated carbon (El Kassimi et al., 2021; Guahk et al., 2021; Meez et al., 2021; Spessato et al., 2021), carbon nanotubes (Abdullah et al., 2021; X. Jiang, Ruan, et al., 2021; Q. Li et al., 2021; Y. Zhao et al., 2021), functionalised conjugate adsorbents (Awual, Yaita, et al., 2020; Kamel et al., 2019), functionalised composites (Awual & Hasan, 2019; Awual, Hasan, et al., 2020; El-Sharkaway et al., 2020) and polysaccharide-based adsorbents (Al-Dhabi et al., 2020; Musarurwa & Tavengwa, 2020; Saad et al., 2021; Salehi & Hosseinifard, 2021; Zeng et al., 2021; T. Zhang et al., 2021). The use of polysaccharide-based adsorbents during adsorptive removal of environmental contaminants is gaining momentum among re­ searchers (Cao et al., 2021; Hu et al., 2020; W. Wang, Ni, et al., 2020). This can be attributed to the fact that polysaccharides such as chitosan and cellulose are relatively cheap and readily available. Chitosan is among the polysaccharide-based adsorbents that have received increasing attention from researchers in recent years (Elanchezhiyan et al., 2021; Kaur et al., 2021; Ma et al., 2021; Sun et al., 2021). It has an edge over most of the conventional adsorbents such as activated carbon because it is readily available and inexpensive (El-Zawahry et al., 2021; Haroun et al., 2021). In addition, chitosan has functionalities such as the amino group $\left(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ and the hydroxyl group (-OH) that act as active sites during the adsorptive removal of pollutants from the environment (Kaur et al., 2021; Ma et al., 2021) (Fig. 1A). Chitosan, however, has relatively low chemical and mechanical stability. The physicochemical properties of chitosan can be enhanced tremendously through the for­ mation of composites with other adsorbents. Many researchers have formed hybrid adsorbents of environmental pollutants using a combi­ nation of chitosan with materials such as graphene oxide (Jamali & Akbari, 2021; X. Jiang, Pan, et al., 2021; Shan et al., 2021), carbon nanotubes (Abdelmonem et al., 2020; Aydin, 2020), polyvinyl alcohol (Attia et al., 2020; He et al., 2020; Khajavian et al., 2020a; Nguyen & Bui, 2021) and metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) (Bahmani et al., 2019; El-Shahat et al., 2020).
Composite adsorbents made from chitosan and MOFs have captured the interests of many researchers (Khajavian et al., 2021; Samuel et al., 2020; P. Shi, Hu, & Duan, 2021; J. Tang, Zhao, et al., 2021; Wen et al., 2020). MOFs are coordination polymers that are highly porous and crystalline. They consist of metal ions or metal clusters and organic li­ gands (Senosy et al., 2021; X. M. Wang, Kou, et al., 2020). The metals that are in common use during the synthesis of MOFs include aluminium, copper, titanium, magnesium, zinc, and zirconium (Dan et al., 2020; Ghiasi et al., 2020; Moinfar et al., 2020; M. Niu et al., 2020). The geometric shape (such as trigonal pyramidal, tetrahedral and octahedral) of the MOFs depends on the coordination number of the central metal. The organic ligands, on the other hand, that are commonly used in MOFs include 1,4-benzene dicarboxylic acid; 1,3,5- benzene tricarboxylic acid; imidicarboxylic acid, and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyldi­ carboxylic acid (Y. Jiang et al., 2020; Lo´pez et al., 2021; Mohammad­ nejad et al., 2018; Xu et al., 2021). The MOFs, formed by combining these organic ligands and metal ions, are efficient adsorbents of a variety of environmental pollutants. Their highly porous nature confers them with a relatively large surface area for the adsorptive removal of con­ taminants from the environment (Duo et al., 2018; M. Y. Fan, Yu, et al., 2021; Ghani et al., 2018; Habila et al., 2020; Wenmin et al., 2020; S. Zhang et al., 2019). Thus, most MOFs have relatively high adsorption capacities for pollutants in the environment. The hybrid materials formed from chitosan and MOFs, therefore have excellent adsorptive properties due to the combination of the good features from the two adsorbents. These composite materials are stabilised by complexation between the functionalities in chitosan and the metal centres of the MOFs as well as interactions such as hydrogen bonds, van der Waals' forces, and $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions. Consequently, many researchers have directed their research efforts towards the development of composite adsorbents using chitosan and MOFs in order to ensure efficient environmental remediation in recent years (Jamshidifard et al., 2019; Liang et al., 2018; L. Liu et al., 2019; Reghioua et al., 2021; Samuel et al., 2020; Zhuo et al., 2017) (Fig. 1B).
There are many recent reviews based on the adsorptive removal of environmental pollutants using chitosan composite adsorbents (Begum et al., 2021; da Silva Alves et al., 2021; Karimi-Maleh et al., 2021; Khademian et al., 2020; Meteku et al., 2020; Nadar et al., 2019; Pal et al., 2021; Yan et al., 2021). For instance, Begum et al. (2021) wrote a detailed review on the application of chitosan composites as adsorbents of heavy metals from the environment. In their paper, they outlined the preparation of chitosan composites as well as their application during the sequestration of heavy metals from environmental samples. In another study, da Silva Alves et al. (2021) reviewed the utilisation of composites of chitosan with magnetic nanoparticles, graphene oxide, carbon nanotubes, activated carbon, and silicon during adsorptive removal of dyes, heavy metals, antibiotics, and other organic pollutants from environmental samples. In the same vein, Karimi-Maleh et al. (2021) explored the adsorption of pharmaceuticals from wastewater using chitosan composite adsorbents. To the best of our knowledge, none of these reviews well-articulated the utilisation of chitosan/MOF composite adsorbents during the adsorptive removal of pollutants from the environment. There are limited comprehensive reviews on adsorp­ tion studies based on these highly porous bio-adsorbents. Thus, the current review aims at exploring the advances in the application of chitosan-based composites during the adsorptive removal of pollutants from food and environmental samples. This study gives complementary information to other reviews based on chitosan/MOF composites such as the one by Nadar et al. (2019). In their study, they explored polysaccharide-based MOFs in general without giving detailed infor­ mation on the application of chitosan/MOFs composite as adsorbents of environmental pollutants. The major premise of this current study, therefore, is to provide focussed and detailed elucidation of the role of chitosan/MOF composites in environmental management. The use of chitosan/MOFs composites minimizes secondary pollution of the envi­ ronment by the adsorbents since they have relatively high adsorption capacities and most are recyclable. Thus, this is an informative review that clearly outlines the positive impact of bio-adsorbents during envi­ ronmental remediation.
## 2. Application of chitosan/MOF hybrid materials during environmental remediation
## 2.1. Chitosan/ZIF hybrid materials
Zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) represent is a special class of metal-organic frameworks composed of imidazolate linkers and metal ions, with structures similar to conventional alumino-silicate zeolites (Chen et al., 2014; Guo et al., 2020) (Fig. 2A). They have intrinsic porous characteristics and abundant functionalities as well as exceptional thermal, chemical and mechanical stabilities (Y. Wang et al., 2019; Yang et al., 2021). These characteristics confer them with good adsorptive properties. As a result, ZIF MOFs have been used to form composites with other materials such as chitosan to form adsorbents with excellent adsorptive properties for environmental pollutants. Their adsorption mechanisms usually involve the formation of hydrogen bonding, pi-pi interactions and electrostatic interactions (Table 2). Composites of chi­ tosan with ZIF generally have relatively higher adsorption capacities, larger surface area and greater mechanical stability as compared to pristine chitosan. This can be attributed to the highly porous nature of ZIFs and the presence of additional functionalities in the MOFs that facilitate the adsorption of pollutants.
Some researchers have used composite materials of chitosan and ZIF MOFs during the adsorptive removal of pollutants from the environment (Bahmani et al., 2019; Guo et al., 2020; Khajavian et al., 2020b; Y. Wang et al., 2019; Yang et al., 2021). For instance, Wang et al. (2019) used ZIF8/chitosan nanocomposites (ZIF-8@CS) for the effective removal of
Congo red dye from industrial wastewater. The ZIF- $8@{\mathsf{C}}S_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ ) nano­ composites were fabricated via the in-situ growth method (Fig. 2B). The stability of the ZIF- $8@{\mathsf{C}}S$ nanocomposite was maintained by chelation between the metal centres of the ZIF-8 MOF and the functionalities in chitosan. Structural elucidation of the ZIF- $8@\mathsf{C}S$ nanocomposite was done using SEM, TEM, XPS, FT-IR, and XRD. They systematically investigated the adsorption behaviour of ZIF- $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{.8@CS}}$ nanocomposites on Congo red in an aqueous solution. The data from adsorption isotherm showed that the adsorption of Congo red by ZIF- $8@{\mathsf{C}}S$ was single-layer adsorption and this was consistent with the Langmuir isotherm model. A relatively high maximum adsorption capacity of $922~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ was ob­ tained under acidic conditions when ZIF- $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{8}}@\mathbf{\boldsymbol{C}}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{S}}$ composite was used for the adsorptive removal of Congo red from wastewater. Congo red is an anionic dye. Under the acidic conditions, protonation of the amino groups in the nanocomposite occurred making it have a net positive charge. Thus, electrostatic interactions facilitated the adsorption of Congo red onto the surface of the ZIF- $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{8}}@\mathbf{\boldsymbol{C}}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{S}}$ nanocomposite. The ki­ netics experiments showed that the adsorption of Congo red fitted the pseudo-second-order model. Thus, the adsorption rate was mainly controlled by the chemisorption mechanism. Congo red has $\pi$ -electrons and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ functionalities that facilitate the formation of $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions and hydrogen bonds, respectively. The adsorptive removal of Congo red from aqueous solutions was, therefore, largely due to electrostatic in­ teractions, hydrogen bonding, $\pi{-}\pi$ conjugation and zinc coordination effect.
Zhao et al. (2020), on the other hand, used ZIF-8/chitosan beads for the removal of tetracycline from water samples. The incorporation of ZIF-8 into the chitosan structure enhanced its adsorption capacity. Consequently, the ZIF-8/chitosan beads they synthesised had a rela­ tively high adsorption capacity $(495.04~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})\nonumber$ ) for tetracycline. The adsorption process of tetracycline by ZIF-8/chitosan beads followed pseudo-second-order and Langmuir models. Their experimental results showed that the extraction efficiency of ZIF-8/chitosan beads for tetracycline was $90\%$ after ten adsorption/desorption cycles. ZIF-8/ chitosan beads are, therefore, reusable and environmentally friendly adsorbents. Structural characterisation results indicated that the mech­ anism of adsorption of tetracycline by ZIF-8/chitosan beads involved electrostatic interaction, $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions and hydrogen bond in­ teractions. These interactions can be attributed to the presence of the hydroxyl and dimethylamine groups in tetracycline that can form hydrogen bonds with the functionalities in the composite beads. In addition, the $\pi$ bonds in the tetracyclic naphthacene carboxamide ring system and the imidazolate linkers in ZIF-8 facilitate the formation of π-π interactions, which help to increase the mechanical strength of the composite beads. Thus, the ZIF-8/chitosan beads are promising green bio-adsorbents that form strong interactions with pharmaceuticals, and they can be recovered easily and are reusable.
In another study, Liu et al. (2019) used ZIF-8/chitosan monolithic composite beads for the adsorption of uranium (VI) from nuclear in­ dustrial wastewater. Monolithic composites, generally, are continuous adsorbents with tractable morphological and physicochemical charac­ teristics (Acquah et al., 2017). The pore sizes of monolithic adsorbents can be easily engineered into either macropores, mesopores or micro­ pores resulting in remarkable tailor-made selectivity. In addition, monolithic adsorbents can be easily fabricated and suitable surface functionalities may be attached to them resulting in enhanced adsorp­ tion capacities (Gaweł et al., 2010). Thus, the ZIF-8/chitosan monolithic composite beads had a relatively high adsorption capacity for uranium of $629~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $\mathsf{p H}3$ . It was an appealing adsorbent from an envi­ ronmental point of view since it could undergo a series of adsorption/ desorption cycles without significant loss of its adsorption capacity of uranium (VI). This can be attributed to the relatively high chemical and mechanical strength of the monolithic composite beads caused by strong chelation between ZIF-8 metal centres with chitosan as well as the presence of effective cross-linkages and hydrogen bonds. Structural elucidation using XPS showed that the adsorption mechanism was mainly through chelation of uranium (VI) with imidazole, hydroxyl and amino groups found in the adsorbent.
ZIF functionalised chitosan composite nanofibers have been used by some researchers during the decontamination of some environmental samples (Bahmani et al., 2019; Mahmoodi et al., 2020). Generally, chitosan nanofibers have a larger surface area and higher adsorption capacity as compared to pristine chitosan. Bahmani et al. (2019), for instance, synthesised chitosan-g-poly (N-vinylcoprolactum)/ZIF-8 (chi­ tosan-g-PNVCL/ZIF-8) nanofibers and successfully used them for the adsorption of chromium (VI), arsenic (V) and phenol from water sam­ ples. The chitosan-g-PNVCL nanofibers were synthesised using free radical polymerization prior to the incorporation of ZIF-8 MOF into their structures. They characterised the chitosan-g-PNVCL/ZIF-8 adsorbent using BET, SEM and XRD. Structural elucidation showed that the incorporation of ZIF-8 into chitosan- $\cdot\mathbf{g}$ -PNVCL nanofibers caused an enhancement of their diameter. Thus, the surface area of the nanofibers increased resulting in an increase in adsorption capacity. Consequently, their experimental results showed that the adsorption capacities of the chitosan-g-PNVCL/ZIF-8 nanofibers for chromium (VI), arsenic (V) and phenol were relatively high and they ranged from 395.8 to $495.6\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $\mathsf{p H}3$ . Acidic conditions promoted the protonation of the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups in the composite nanofibers resulting in the formation of positive charges. Electrostatic interactions between these positive charges and the negative charges on the arsenate, dichromate and phenoxide ions facilitated their adsorption. In a similar study, Mahmoodi et al. (2020) synthesised a pearl necklace-like ZIF- $\{\boldsymbol{{\8}}\ @$ chitosan/poly(vinyl alcohol) (PVA) nanofiber and used it for the adsorption of dye from aqueous solutions. The ZIF-8@chitosan/PVA nanofiber was synthesised by coating ZIF-8 on chitosan/PVA electrospun nanofiber. Their experi­ mental results also showed that this adsorbent had a relatively high adsorption capacity of $1000\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for the dye.
Yang et al. (2021) successfully used nano-ZIF- $\cdot8@$ chitosan micro­ spheres $\left(\mathbf{ZIF-}8@\mathbf{CSM}\right)$ for the adsorptive removal of p-hydroxybenzoic acid from agro-industrial wastewater. p-Hydroxybenzoic acid is an emerging pollutant that has multiple sources such as the hydrolysis of methylparaben, ethylparaben and propylparaben used widely as pre­ servatives in food and drinks. The presence of p-hydroxybenzoic acid in environmental water poses health problems to living organisms. Thus, its effective removal from water sources is imperative and ZIF- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot8@\mathbf{C}S\mathbf{M}$ is a promising adsorbent that can help to achieve this end. Yang et al. (2021), in their study, fabricated ZIF- $8@\mathrm{CSM}$ using a combination of inverse emulsion method and in-situ growth method (Fig. 2C). The adsorption kinetic experiments showed that adsorption of p-hydrox­ ybenzoic acid by the ZIF- $8@\mathrm{CSM}$ adsorbent rapidly reached equilibrium in about $30\ s.$ . The equilibrium data fitted well into the Langmuir isothermal model. Experimental results showed that ZIF-8@CSM had a relatively high maximum adsorption capacity of $128.70~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ for phydroxybenzoic acid.
The use of chitosan/MOFs composite adsorbents in aquatic envi­ ronments is usually riddled by biofouling. Thus, there is a need to develop chitosan/MOFs composite adsorbents with anti-fouling prop­ erties and Guo et al. (2020) performed research to this effect. They developed a chitosan-graphene oxide/ZIF (GCZ8A) foam adsorbent with antifouling properties via in situ growth of silver ions doped ZIF-8 on a chitosan-graphene oxide foam substrate and successfully used it as an adsorbent of uranium in seawater. The composite adsorbent they syn­ thesised was relatively stable. Its stability can be attributed to strong interactions between graphene oxide, chitosan and ZIF-8 MOF. Complexation of the metal centres of ZIF-8 with the carboxyl groups in graphene oxide and the amino groups in chitosan could have contrib­ uted to the extra stability. In addition, hydrogen bonds and $\pi{-}\pi$ in­ teractions helped to keep the composite intact. Thus, the GCZ8A composite adsorbent exhibited high mechanical properties with about 352.41 KPa of compression stress and a low degradation rate with $3.26\%$ of mass loss in seawater. The adsorbent, due to the presence of silver ions with ant-microbial properties, inhibited the growth of Nitzschia and their cell death rate was more than $70\%$ . In addition, the GCZ8A com­ posites inhibited cell adhesion on its surface. Thus, the GCZ8A foam adsorbent had remarkable anti-biofouling properties. On the other hand, the GCZ8A foam adsorbent had a relatively high maximum adsorption capacity of $361.01\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}}$ for uranium and this was 1.9 times higher than that of pure ZIF-8 $(189.75\mathrm{~mg~g~-~}1)$ at $\mathrm{pH}8.0$ . Under basic con­ ditions deprotonation of the acidic groups in the composite occurred resulting in the formation of negative charges, which then electrostati­ cally attracted the positively charged uranium ions. Structural elucida­ tion experiments showed that the GCZ8A foam adsorbent had a relatively large specific surface area of $200.79\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ as well as nitro­ gen/oxygen functional groups. These are the attributes that can be used to explain its relatively high adsorption capacity. Another attractive feature of the GCZ8A adsorbent was that it could undergo a series of regeneration cycles without significant loss of its adsorption capacity as a result of its high mechanical stability.
## 2.2. Chitosan/MIL hybrid materials
MIL (Materials of Institute Lavoisier) MOFs are coordination poly­ mers composed of trivalent metal nodes (such as chromium (III), va­ nadium(III) and iron(III)) complexed with porous carboxylate ligands (Elrasheedy et al., 2019; Ghiasi et al., 2020) (Fig. 3A). They possess significantly large channels/cages with topologies similar to zeolites. These MIL MOFs have an open-framework structure, and their pore sizes and shapes depend on the strength of the host-guest interactions. They have relatively high chemical and mechanical stabilities (Moinfar et al., 2020; Senosy et al., 2021). The physicochemical properties of adsor­ bents such as chitosan can be improved by incorporating MIL MOFs into their structures. This results in the formation of highly porous adsor­ bents with multi-functionalities and improved mechanical strength. These features make MIL composites to be suitable for use as adsorbents of pollutants in food and environmental samples. Thus, hybrid materials made from chitosan and MIL MOFs are slowly gaining popularity among researchers during the adsorptive removal of pollutants from the envi­ ronment (El-Shahat et al., 2020; L. Liu et al., 2016; Y. Wang, Wang, et al., 2020; Xiong et al., 2020) (Table 1). Chitosan/MIL-based adsor­ bents form many types of interactions such as hydrogen bonding, elec­ trostatic interactions, pi-pi interactions and cation-pi interactions with environmental pollutants during adsorption (Table 2).
One of the MIL MOFs used by researchers is MIL-101 (Y. Wang, Wang, et al., 2020; Xiong et al., 2020). It is a mesoporous MOF that is composed of chromium or iron has a metal centre and terephthalate as the organic linker. MIL-101 has a large surface area and pore volume as well as having fairly good resistance to common solvents and thermal stability (Darvishnejad et al., 2020; Ghiasi et al., 2020). Its framework consists of terephthalate linkers and $\bf{M}_{3}0$ -carboxylate trimers $\mathbf{\hat{M}}=\mathbf{Cr}$ or Fe) with octahedrally coordinated metal ions binding terminal water molecules. The water ligands can be removed from the MIL-101 MOF by thermal treatment and this creates active unsaturated coordinate sites, which can be used during complexation with other adsorbents such as chitosan to form composites (Maksimchuk et al., 2012). The incorpo­ ration of MIL-101 into the chitosan structure enhances its chemical and thermal stability as well as the introduction of additional functionalities that aid the adsorption process of pollutants from the environment.
Chitosan sponge adsorbents are highly porous and present a large surface area during the adsorptive removal of pollutants from the environment. The porosity of chitosan sponges can be further enhanced by the formation of composites with MOFs such as MIL-101. In addition, chitosan/MIL-101 sponge adsorbents have relatively high mechanical stabilities and high adsorption capacities as well as being multifunc­ tional. These properties make chitosan/MIL-101 sponges good adsor­ bents of environmental pollutants. Wang, Kou, et al. (2020), for instance, successfully used a nano-MIL-101(Fe)@chitosan hybrid sponge to remove anionic dyes from aqueous solutions. Chelation be­ tween the unsaturated coordinate sites in MIL-101(Fe) and the $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{2}}$ groups in chitosan helped to stabilise the composite as well as hydrogen bonds. The hybrid sponge composite had a porous structure and high elasticity as well as good mechanical stability. These characteristics conferred it with good adsorption capacity and re-usability. The hybrid polymeric sponge adsorbent was prepared using a freeze-drying method and then characterised using SEM, TEM and FT-IR. The characterisation results showed that the rhombic MIL-101(Fe) particles were embedded into the hybrid sponge uniformly. The adsorption efficiency of the hybrid sponge for the anionic dyes was relatively high as compared with the pristine chitosan sponge. This can be attributed to the presence of additional functionalities as well as the improved surface area from the porous MIL-101(Fe) MOF. Thus, the hybrid sponge adsorbent had rela­ tively high adsorption capacities for the anionic dyes. For instance, the maximum adsorption capacity of MIL-101(Fe) $@$ chitosan sponge for acid red 94 was $4518\ \mathrm{\mg\8}^{-1}$ . The adsorption mechanisms mainly involved electrostatic interactions and hydrogen bonding.
In the same vein, Liu et al. (2016) prepared a magnetic composite bio-adsorbent using chitosan, iron oxide nanoparticles and MIL-101(Cr) MOF $\mathrm{(m{-}C S/F e_{3}O_{4}/M I L{-}101)}$ . The composite was also stabilised by complexation between the functionalities in chitosan and the unsatu­ rated sites in the MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ structure. The $\mathrm{m{-}C S/F e_{3}O_{4}/M I L{-}}101$ composite was characterised using XRD, BET and vibrating sample magnetometer prior to its application during the adsorptive removal of methyl orange dye from aqueous solutions. The characterisation results showed that the composite adsorbent had a large surface area for the adsorption of methyl orange dye and enough magnetism to enable it to be retrieved from the bulk solution using an external magnet. Electro­ static interactions and hydrogen bonds were involved during the adsorptive removal of methyl orange from water. The bio-adsorbent had a relatively high maximum adsorption capacity of $117.6\mathrm{\mg\}8^{-1}$ for methyl orange. This can be attributed to the presence of the MIL-101 MOF, which has a highly porous structure and a large surface area. The enhanced adsorption capacity was indicative of the high extraction efficiency of the $\mathrm{m{-}C S/F e_{3}O_{4}/M I L{-}}101$ composite for methyl orange. In addition, the composite bio-adsorbent was reusable several times without significant loss of its adsorption capacity due to increased me­ chanical strength from the MIL-101(Fe) MOF and it could be recovered easily from the bulk solution using an external magnetic field. These attributes made $\mathrm{m{-}C S/F e_{3}O_{4}/M I L{-}}101$ bio-adsorbent to be more appealing to environmentally conscious researchers.
In a similar study, Zhuo et al. (2017) successfully used composite beads (MIL-101(Cr)/CS) made from chitosan and MIL-101(Cr) MOF for the adsorption of pharmaceuticals and personal care products (PPCPs) from water. The stability of the composite beads was maintained by complexation between the functionalities of the PPCPs and the metal centres of MIL-101 as well as electrostatic interactions and hydrogen bonds. They studied the adsorptive removal of three PPCPs, namely benzoic acid, ibuprofen and ketoprofen, from water using the MIL-101 $({\mathrm{Cr}})/{\mathrm{CS}}$ composite beads. The composite beads exhibited relatively high adsorption capacity for the PPCPs as compared to pristine chitosan. The adsorptive properties of the composite were enhanced by the incorporation of the highly porous MIL-101 MOF into the chitosan structure. Structural elucidation using X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy showed that the extraction of the PPCPs from wastewater occurred through electrostatic interactions between the protonated amino groups of chitosan and the $\mathrm{cr}$ centres of the MIL-101(Cr) MOF. Adsorption isotherms and $\pi$ -energy analysis showed that $\pi-\pi$ interaction of the aromatic groups between adsorbents and contaminants was another possible mechanism during the adsorptive removal of PPCPs from wastewater. They also observed that the MIL-101(Cr)/CS composite beads could undergo a series of adsorption/desorption cycles without a significant decrease in adsorption capacity. Thus, the $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr})/\mathrm{CS}$ composite beads are reusable, as a result of enhanced mechanical strength, and environmentally friendly adsorbents.
Some researchers used MIL-125 MOF to fabricate chitosan and suc­ cessfully used the composites during the adsorptive removal of pollut­ ants from environmental samples. MIL-125 was first reported by DanHardi et al. (2009) and was synthesised using titanium(IV) isopropoxide as the metal cluster and 1,4-benzene dicarboxylic acid as the organic linker. It is thermally stable up to $290^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ highly porous and has a large surface area. These attractive features make it have good adsorptive properties for environmental pollutants. Thus, the fabrication of other adsorbents such as chitosan with MIL-125 results in the enhancement of their physicochemical properties. Liang et al. (2018), for instance, used composite beads composed of MIL-125 MOF and chitosan (MIL-125/CS)
as an adsorbent of lead (II) ions from wastewater. The MIL-125/CS composite beads were synthesised by combining MIL-125 and chitosan using a template-free solvothermal method under ambient conditions. The use of ambient conditions during its synthesis, made the MIL-125/ CS adsorbent appealing from an environmental point of view. Struc­ tural characterisation of the beads was done using FT-IR, XPS, SEM and TGA. The characterisation results showed that the MIF-125 fillers were successfully embedded into the chitosan matrix. In addition, they showed that MIL-125/CS had relatively high thermal stability. This can be attributed to the presence of the thermally stable MIL-125 MOF in its structure. The maximum adsorption capacity of the composite beads for lead (II) ions was relatively high $(407.50\mathrm{\mg\g^{-1})}$ at ambient tempera­ ture. This can be explained in terms of the highly porous nature and large surface area of the adsorbent due to the presence of the MIL-125 particles embedded into the composite. The adsorption of the $\mathbf{Pb^{2+}}$ by the MIL-125/CS was favoured by alkaline conditions. Under basic con­ ditions, deprotonation of the acidic groups in the composite would occur resulting in the formation of negative charges. Electrostatic interactions between the negative groups in the composite and the positively charged lead (II) facilitated the adsorption process. They performed a series of adsorption/desorption cycles for lead (II) ions using MIL-125/CS com­ posite beads as adsorbents and there was no significant decrease in adsorption capacity. The enhanced mechanical stability of the MIL-125/ CS composite made it remain relatively intact during each adsorption/ desorption cycle. The MIL-125/CS composite beads are, therefore, reusable and very appealing from an environmentally point of view.
In the same vein, El-Shahat et al. (2020) successfully used a mem­ brane synthesised from $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and cross-linked chitosan for the adsorptive removal of iodide ions from radioactive wastewater. The incorporation of MIL- $125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and cross-linkages greatly enhanced the chemical and thermal stability of the chitosan membrane. The chitosan films were synthesised using solvent casting technique while the chito­ san/MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ composite films were prepared by post-synthetic modification of the chitosan membrane. The composite was stabilised by strong hydrogen bonds between the functionalities in chitosan and $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ MOF as well as complexation between the $\mathrm{T}^{4+}$ metal centres and the $\mathrm{OH}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups in the polysaccharide. The synthesised chitosan/MIL- $125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ membrane was characterised using SEM, FT-IR, BET, XRD and TGA. The SEM image of crosslinked chitosan revealed that it had hexagonal porous surfaces (Fig. 3B). The presence of MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ in the structure of crosslinked chitosan resulted in the enhancement of its pore sizes (Fig. 3C) as well as its specific surface area. The adsorption of iodide ions by this membrane adsorbent occurred through electrostatic interactions after the protonation of the $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ group under acidic conditions as well as chemosorption via host-guest interactions. A relatively high adsorption capacity of $399.68\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ was obtained when the chitosan $/\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ membrane was used for the adsorptive removal of iodide ions from wastewater. The enriched chitosan/MIL$125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ membrane was immersed in $0.5\mathrm{~M~}$ NaCl solution during desorption and stirred overnight at room temperature. After filtering off the NaCl solution, the membrane was washed several times with deionised water until no iodine could be detected using UV–vis spec­ trophotometer. The membrane was reusable for several times without significant loss of its adsorption capacity.
MIL-100(Fe) is another MOF that has excellent adsorptive properties. It can be synthesised easily using the microwave-assisted hydrothermal method, which involves the spontaneous coordination between iron(III) cations and 1,3,5-benzene tricarboxylate linkers resulting in the gener­ ation of a porous architecture (Cutrone et al., 2019). The MIL-100(Fe) structure is accessible through the 5.6 and $8.6\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ mesoporous windows and it forms 24 and $29\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ internal cages. These mesoporous windows and internal cages increase the surface area of MIL-100(Fe). In addition, it forms a significant amount of accessible Lewis acid metal sites upon dehydration (Hei et al., 2014). These unoccupied sites are available during chemical modification and formation of composites with other adsorbents such as chitosan. Thus, some researchers have taken advantage of these unique properties of MIL-100(Fe) and used it to form versatile composite adsorbents with multi-functionalities and enhanced mechanical strength. Xiong et al. (2020), for instance, used nano-MIL100(Fe) modified chitosan for the adsorption of antimony (III) from aqueous solutions. The MIL-100(Fe)/chitosan adsorbent was prepared by grafting iron oxide nanoparticles and MIL-100(Fe) onto chitosan prior to characterisation using FT-IR and XRD. Optimum conditions for the removal of antimony (III) from aqueous solutions were evaluated using batch experiments. Their results indicated that the adsorption efficiency for antimony (III) increased with an increase in the concen­ tration of the MIL-100(Fe)/chitosan adsorbent as well as an increase in $\mathtt{p H}$ . They obtained a maximum adsorption efficiency of $96.8\%$ using a $10\mathrm{\mg\L^{-1}}$ solution of antimony (III) and an adsorbent with a concen­ tration of $15\mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ at a $\mathsf{p H}$ of 11. Thus, their findings showed that MIL100(Fe)/chitosan adsorbent could be used for the effective adsorptive removal of antimony (III) from aqueous solutions. In addition, the MILmodified chitosan adsorbent could be easily recovered and reused several times without significant loss of its adsorption capacity.
## 2.3. Chitosan/UiO hybrid materials
UiO (University of Olso) are co-ordination polymers that belong to the zirconium–carboxylate-based MOF family. UiO-66, UiO-67 and UiO68 are among the common zirconium-based MOFs that are used for the adsorptive removal of pollutants from the environment (Duo et al., 2018; Mao et al., 2019; Mehta et al., 2019) (Fig. 4A). They possess substantial chemical stability and exceptional thermal stability (Elrasheedy et al., 2019). In addition, they are highly porous and have good mechanical stability. UiO-66, however, has been widely used by researchers as an adsorbent for environmental pollutants since it has outstanding stability as compared to the other UiO MOFs (Cao et al., 2021; Jamshidifard et al., 2019; Wen et al., 2020) (Table 1). This remarkable stability may be attributed to the compact structure of UiO66 as well as the strong Zr–O bonds (Ru et al., 2021). These properties make UiO-66 suitable for use during the formation of composite ad­ sorbents with materials such as chitosan. Its unique pore sizes confer composite adsorbents with some selectivity during the adsorptive removal of environmental pollutants.
Hybrid materials composed of chitosan and UiO-66 MOFs have been applied during the adsorptive removal of some pollutants from envi­ ronmental samples (Jamshidifard et al., 2019; Y. Wang et al., 2021; Wen et al., 2020). For instance, Wen et al. (2020) successfully used chitosan/ UiO-66 MOF composite monolith as an adsorbent for the removal of Congo red dye from wastewater. The composite monolith was prepared using the ice-templating method. Chelation between the metal centres of the UiO-66 MOF and the functionalities in chitosan helped to keep the composite monolith stable. The stability of the monolith in aqueous solutions was also enhanced by cross-linking UiO-66 MOF with glutar­ aldehyde prior to its formation. After synthesis, the monolith was characterised using SEM, $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption, and XRD. These structural characterisation techniques revealed that the composite monolith possessed hierarchical pores with UiO-66 particles embedded on the surface of the chitosan matrix. The presence of hierarchical pores conferred the composite monolith with a large surface area for the adsorption of the Congo red dye. The monolith managed to adsorb about $90\%$ of the Congo red dye from aqueous solutions in $60\mathrm{min}$ . Thus, the adsorption efficiency of the monolith was very high with a maximum adsorption capacity of $246.21~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .
Jamshidifard et al. (2019) incorporated amino-functionalised UiO$66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ MOF into polyacrylonitrile (PAN)/chitosan nanofibers and used the resultant composite for the adsorption and membrane filtration of lead (II), cadmium (II) and chromium (VI) ions from aqueous solutions. The amino-functionalised UiO-66 was synthesised by microwave heating method using zirconium chloride, $^{N,N}$ -dimethylformamide, 2- aminoterephthalic acid and hydrochloric acid followed by drying at room temperature for $24\mathrm{h}$ (Fig. 4B). The UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ MOF formed was then incorporated into a mixture of solutions of PAN and chitosan resulting in the formation of UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ /PAN/chitosan nanofibers. The stability of the nanofibrous membrane was maintained by complexation, electrostatic interactions and hydrogen bonds. Structural characterisa­ tion of the nanofibers was done using XRD, BET, FT-IR and SEM. The SEM images of PAN/chitosan nanofibers revealed that the adsorbent had a smooth morphology with an average diameter of the nanofibers being $235\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 4C). The incorporation of UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ into PAN/chitosan nanofibers caused them to have a well-defined morphology and there was also an enhancement of diameter to about $345~\mathrm{{nm}}$ (Fig. 4D). Sub­ sequently, the nanofibers were used for the adsorption of lead (II), cadmium (II) and chromium (VI) ions from aqueous solutions. The in­ clusion of $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ greatly enhanced the adsorption capacities of PAN/chitosan nanofibrous adsorbent due to its highly porous nature and large surface area. The lead (II), cadmium (II) and chromium (VI) adsorption capacities were increased from 87.5, 83 and $78~\mathrm{mg}.8^{-1}$ to 115, 107.6 and $99.5~\mathrm{mg}.8^{-1}$ , respectively by loading $10\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ MOF into the PAN/chitosan nanofibrous adsorbent. The long syn­ thetic procedure for the adsorbent, however, is a cause of concern from an environmental point of view since it increases the risk of secondary pollution of the environment.
Wang et al. (2021) successfully used porous membrane synthesised from crosslinked chitosan, polyvinyl alcohol and UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (cCS/ PVA/UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH_{2}}$ ) membrane adsorbent during the adsorptive removal of uranium (VI) ions from radioactive wastewater. The presence of the UiO-66 MOF and cross-linkages greatly enhanced the mechanical sta­ bility of the chitosan membrane adsorbent. Experimental results revealed that the cCS/PVA/UiO-66- $\mathbf{\cdotNH}_{2}$ membrane adsorbent had a relatively high adsorption capacity of $316.50\ \mathrm{mg\g^{-1}}$ under optimum conditions. The relatively high adsorption capacity can be explained in terms of the presence of the highly porous UiO-66 MOF with a large BET surface area in the membrane structure. They also observed that the membrane adsorbent exhibited high selectivity for the uranium ions in wastewater. The high selectivity can be attributed to the unique pore sizes conferred to the membrane due to the presence of UiO-66 MOF in chitosan. The adsorptive removal of uranium (VI) ions from wastewater occurs mainly through surface complexation and cation-exchange mechanisms. The cCS/PVA/UiO-66- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ membrane adsorbent was relatively stable and consequently, it could undergo a series of adsorption-desorption cycles without significant loss in its adsorption capacity. The membrane is, therefore, reusable and this made it to be more appealing from an environmental point of view.
## 2.4. Chitosan/HKUST hybrid materials
HKUST-1 or copper-1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate (Cu-BTC) is an MOF composed of copper (II) ion nodes and benzene tricarboxylate (BTC) linkers (Doma´n et al., 2021; Vepsa¨la¨inen et al., 2021) (Fig. 5A). It was first synthesised by Chui et al. (1999) using a solvothermal method. From that time other synthetic techniques such as the mechanochemical method (Klimakow et al., 2010) and continuous flow (Dunne et al., 2016) have been successfully used by researchers during the production of HKUST-1. It has a relatively high surface area of $600{-}1600\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , a large pore volume of approximately $0.7\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and good thermal stability (up to $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ (Bouson et al., 2017). These attractive properties of HKUST-1 make it suitable for use as an adsorbent of environmental pollutants. Its use as an adsorbent, however, is hampered by the fact that it has lower water stability and poor mechanical strength. The chemical and mechanical stability of HKUST can be improved by cross-linking and the formation of composites with other adsorbents such as graphene oxide and cellulose.
Composites of chitosan and HKUST MOFs have been used by some researchers as adsorbents during adsorption since they form strong interactions with environmental pollutants. (Q. Liu, Yu, et al., 2021; Samuel et al., 2020; Zhao et al., 2020) (Table 1). The interactions be­ tween chitosan/HKUST adsorbent and environmental pollutants usually involve the formation of electrostatic interactions, hydrogen bonding and $\pi-\pi$ interactions (Table 2). Chitosan/HKUST composites result in the formation of multi-functional adsorbents with relatively high adsorption capacities. The mechanical strengths of chitosan/HKUST-1 composites, however, are relatively low. Thus, there is a need to incorporate into the composites other adsorbents such as cellulose with high mechanical stabilities. For instance, Liu, Yu, et al. (2021) used HKUST-1/cellulose/chitosan composite aerogel for the adsorptive removal of methylene blue from industrial wastewater. Chitosan aero­ gels have large specific surface area $(149{-}739\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ (Lovskaya et al., 2020). The incorporation of HKUST MOF into the chitosan aerogels caused an improvement of their specific surface area. This can be attributed to the highly porous nature of the HKUST MOF. The me­ chanical stability of the composite aerogel was enhanced by the incor­ poration of cellulose into its structure as well as by cross-linking. The aerogel was prepared using in-situ growth that involved the combina­ tion of covalent cross-linking, vacuum freeze-drying and solvothermal methods (Fig. 5B). The incorporation of cellulose did not only confer the aerogel with good stability but it also enabled the composite to maintain fine morphology and structure under acidic conditions. The BET surface area of the HKUST/cellulose/chitosan aerogel $(457.75\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ was larger than that of the cellulose/chitosan aerogel $(9.74\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . Thus, as already alluded to, the inclusion of the HKUST MOF extensively enhanced the surface area of the aerogel. Consequently, the adsorptive removal of methylene blue from wastewater was characterised by a relatively high adsorption capacity of $526.3\mathrm{~mg~}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . In addition, HKUST/cellulose/chitosan aerogel had good recycling capability due to enhanced mechanical stability and this made it to be more appealing from an environmental point of view.
Some researchers have incorporated graphene oxide into chito­ san@HKUST composites during the adsorptive removal of environ­ mental pollutants. This can be attributed to the fact that graphene oxide has relatively high chemical and mechanical stability. Thus, its inclusion into chitosan@HKUST composites greatly improved the physicochem­ ical properties of the adsorbents (Papageorgiou et al., 2017). In addition, graphene oxide has oxygen functionalities that enabled it to be dispersed easily in different chemical environments (Dideikin & Vul”, 2019). Consequently, it can be combined easily with other materials such as chitosan to form adsorbents with enhanced mechanical stabilities and relatively high adsorption capacities. For instance, Samuel et al. (2020) used HKUST MOF immobilized on graphene oxide-chitosan hybrid composite for the adsorption of methylene blue from aqueous solutions. They characterised the adsorbent using XRD, FTIR, SEM, TEM and BET.
The structural characterisation of the adsorbent showed that chitosan and HKUST were embedded into the structure of graphene oxide. The adsorption of methylene blue by the graphene oxide-chitosan $@$ HKUST composite was modelled using Langmuir isotherm and pseudo-secondorder kinetic model. The adsorbent had a relatively large BET surface area and rough morphology. The large surface area can be attributed to the highly porous nature of HKUST-1. Consequently, its adsorption ca­ pacity for methylene blue $(357.15\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}}\cdot$ ) was relatively high. The combination of HKUST-1, chitosan and graphene oxide resulted in the formation of a multi-functional adsorbent, which could form strong electrostatic and $\pi-\pi$ interactions with methylene blue.
In another study, Jiamjirangkul et al. (2020) used HKUST-integrated chitosan/polyvinyl alcohol (PVA) nanofibrous membrane for the selec­ tive capture and filtration of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ from the environment. The HKUSTintegrated chitosan/PVA nanofibrous membrane was synthesised by layer-by-layer deposition of $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ as metal cluster and BTC as the organic precursor to form HKUST MOF on the surface of the chitosan/PVA nanofiber. The hybrid nanofibrous membrane formed had a relatively high surface area due to the presence of many functional groups in its structure. Consequently, the adsorption capacity of the membrane for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ was relatively high. Their experimental results showed that the specific surface area and the pore size distribution of the hybrid mem­ brane could be controlled by changing the order the precursors were mixed. The specific surface area of the membrane was greatly enhanced by immersing the chitosan/PVA nanofibrous membranes into the metal solution prior to the organic solution. The resultant HKUST/chitosan/ PVA nanofibrous membrane exhibited great potential for the selective capture and filtration of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ in the environment.
## 3. Challenges and future prospects
Chitosan/MOF composite adsorbents have excellent physicochem­ ical properties essential for the adsorptive removal of environmental contaminants (P. Shi, Hu, & Duan, 2021; J. Tang, Zhao, et al., 2021). They are highly porous adsorbents with multi-functional groups that act as active sites for the adsorption of pollutants from environmental samples (El-Shahat et al., 2020; Mahmoodi et al., 2020). Thus, chitosan/ MOF adsorbents generally have relatively high adsorption capacities for both organic and inorganic pollutants. In addition, chitosan can form stable complexes with metal ions in MOFs and this facilitates the inte­ gration of the two (Nadar et al., 2019). Despite these attractive features, the use of chitosan/MOF adsorbents have some challenges.
Chitosan has very low solubility in water and this has a negative impact during the formation of composites with MOFs. This is due to the fact that the conversion of chitosan to a water-soluble form is imperative during composite formation. Neufeld et al. (2017), for instance, had to react chitosan with acetic acid in order to convert it to a water-soluble form prior to composing it with MOF. This has the effect of increasing the production cost of the chitosan/MOF composites. On the other hand, the stability of some MOFs is a cause of concern during the synthesis of chitosan/MOF composites. For instance, HKUST MOF has generally low mechanical strength. Thus, materials such as graphene oxide should be incorporated into the chitosan/HKUST composites in order to make them mechanically stable. The incorporation of graphene oxide into the composite makes it to be expensive.
The chitosan/MOF composite adsorbents are prone to fouling (organic fouling, colloidal fouling, and biofouling) during the adsorptive removal of pollutants in aquatic environments (Das et al., 2013). Fouling reduces the pore sizes and surface roughness of the chitosan/MOF ad­ sorbents as well as blockage of surface functional groups (Das et al., 2013; Guo et al., 2020). This has negative effects on the extraction ef­ ficiencies of the composite adsorbents. Thus, concerted research efforts should be directed towards the development of smart adsorbents with anti-fouling properties. Biofouling is among the most problematic forms of fouling when chitosan/MOF composite adsorbents are used for the adsorptive removal of pollutants in aquatic environments. This predicament can be minimised by the fabrication of nanoparticles with anti-microbial activities into the structures of chitosan/MOF composites (Guo et al., 2020).
Selectivity of the chitosan/MOF composite adsorbents is a cause of concern when used during adsorptive removal of environmental pol­ lutants. Any pollutant with complimentary functionalities as those found on the chitosan/MOF adsorbent can be adsorbed by the com­ posite. Selectivity can be greatly enhanced if the composite is formed using molecularly printed chitosan and MOFs. This enables the forma­ tion of tailor-made cavities in the adsorbents that can selectively adsorb specific pollutants from contaminated environments. In addition, incorporation of other multi-functional polymeric adsorbents such as polydopamine (T. Zhang et al., 2021) and conjugated materials (Awual et al., 2019; Kamel et al., 2019) into the chitosan/MOF composites may result in the enhancement of the adsorption capacities of the adsorbents.
Chitosan/MOF composites are promising adsorbents for environ­ mental pollutants. Their use on a large scale, however, is hampered by the fact that the synthetic routes for most of these composites are very long and this makes the process uneconomical. To pave way for indus­ trial application, more research efforts should be directed towards establishing alternative synthetic routes for these composites that are short and less expensive. In addition, there is a need to scale-up research efforts towards the development of more chitosan/MOF composites that can be easily recovered and recycled. Thus, the lack of high-throughput synthesis and cheap regeneration methods for the chitosan/MOF ad­ sorbents are among the major drawbacks for their large-scale applications.
## 4. Conclusion
Chitosan/MOF hybrid materials are efficient adsorbents for both organic and inorganic pollutants in environmental samples. The incor­ poration of MOFs into chitosan enhances tremendously its adsorption capacity of a myriad of environmental pollutants. This can be attributed to the highly porous nature of the MOFs that increases the adsorptive surface area of the hybrid adsorbent. The inclusion of the MOFs also improves the mechanical properties of the composite adsorbents and this confers them with low degradation rates. Thus, most chitosan/MOF adsorbents can undergo many adsorption/desorption cycles without significant loss of adsorption capacity. Desorption of the pollutants from the enriched adsorbents is usually done using organic solvents and there is a risk of secondary pollution of the environment. Thus, there is a need to develop more physical methods, such as thermal and magnetic retrieval, for the recovery of pollutants from the enriched adsorbents. In addition, many chitosan/MOF adsorbents, generally, have high thermal and chemical stabilities. The adsorptive removal of environmental pol­ lutants usually occurs via the following mechanisms: hydrogen bonding, electrostatic interactions, and $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions.
